  "stemBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "The diagram shows the straight line L. (a) State the y-intercept of L. (b) State the coordinates of the point at which L cuts the x-axis. (c) Determine the gradient of L. (d) Write the equation of L in the form y = mx + c. (e) Use the graph to determine the value of y when x = −1."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Think first: The line slopes downwards, so expect a negative gradient before you calculate."
    },
    {
      "type": "asset",
      "storagePath": "assets/question_visuals/p073_q11.jpg",
      "altText": "Source visual for edmar-csec-p073-q11 from workbook page 73."
    }
  ],
  "solutionSteps": [
    {
      "stepNo": 1,
      "instruction": "Read where the line crosses the y-axis.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "The line meets the vertical axis 4 units above the origin"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y-intercept = 4, the point (0, 4)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 2,
      "instruction": "Read where the line crosses the x-axis.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "The line meets the horizontal axis 2 units to the right of the origin"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: (2, 0)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 3,
      "instruction": "Substitute the two points into the gradient formula.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "m = (0 − 4)/(2 − 0) = −4/2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: m = −2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 4,
      "instruction": "Write the equation.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "y = mx + c with m = −2 and c = 4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y = −2x + 4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 5,
      "instruction": "Find y when x = −1.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Read up from x = −1 to the line, and check: y = −2(−1) + 4 = 2 + 4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y = 6"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "strategyBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Read both intercepts from the diagram, compute the gradient, write the equation, then use it or the graph to find y at x = −1."
    }
  ],
  "finalAnswerBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "(a) 4  (b) (2, 0)  (c) gradient = −2  (d) y = −2x + 4  (e) y = 6"
    }
  ],
  "whyThisWorks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "A negative gradient means y decreases as x increases, which is exactly what a line falling from left to right shows. The value −2 says that every step of 1 to the right drops the line 2 units, and that constant rate is what keeps the graph straight. Once the equation is known, reading a value off the graph and substituting into the equation must agree, so each acts as a check on the other."
    }
  ],
